Q: I made a simple crash algorithm, can you take a look at it? A: <pre><code>auto start1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        volatile float dummy1;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; iterations; ++i) {
        dummy1 = std::sqrt(x * x + y * y);
        }
        auto end1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        std::chrono::duration&lt;double&gt; diff1 = end1 - start1;
</code></pre>
<p>This code does nothing.  Because x and y do not vary, any half decent compiler will optimize the for loop away ( you are, of course, compiling with optimization on )</p>
<p>To make a worthwhile performance test, you need to store random values of x, y in a vector and extract each pair inside the loop.</p>
<p>You have lebelled this code 'standard method'.  It is not. Performant collision detection code does not extract square roots ( horribly expensive ).  Rather it compares squares.  You need to include the comparison in your loop.</p>

Q: cursor won&#39;t move when using move() or wmove() when using the curses library A: <p>I suspect that you are trying to print outside the bounds of the window.</p>

<p>In particular, I would guess that here:</p>

<pre><code>mvwprintw(results_scrn,num_rows_res,(num_col_res/2) - 2,"Close");
</code></pre>

<p>...<code>num_rows_res</code> is the number of rows in the <code>results_scrn</code> window - but that means that the valid row coordinates range from <code>0</code> to <code>num_rows_res - 1</code>.</p>

<p>If you try to <code>move()</code> or <code>wmove()</code> outside the window, the cursor will not actually move; a subsequent <code>printw()</code> or <code>wprintw()</code> will print at the previous cursor position.  If you try to <code>mvprintw()</code> or <code>mvwprintw()</code>, the whole call will fail at the point of trying to move the cursor, and so it won't print anything at all.</p>

<p>Here's a complete demonstration (just printing to <code>stdscr</code> which has <code>LINES</code> rows and <code>COLS</code> columns):</p>

<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;curses.h&gt;

int main(void)
{
    int ch;

    initscr();
    noecho();
    cbreak();

    /* This succeeds: */
    mvprintw(1, 1, "&gt;&gt;&gt;");

    /* This tries to move outside the window, and fails before printing: */
    mvprintw(LINES, COLS / 2, "doesn't print at all");

    /* This tries to move outside the window, and fails: */
    move(LINES, COLS / 2);

    /* This prints at the cursor (which hasn't successfully moved yet): */
    printw("prints at current cursor");

    /* This is inside the window, and works: */
    mvprintw(LINES - 1, COLS / 2, "prints at bottom of screen");

    refresh();
    ch = getch();
    endwin();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

<p>(In fact the functions do return a result; if you check it, you'll find that it's <code>ERR</code> in the cases that fail.)</p>

Q: A virtual function with the same name and signature in two base classes A: <p>Here is other way it can be addressed (not adding more virtual functions):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct SlotA
{
    virtual void func(int value) = 0;
};

struct SlotB
{
    virtual void func(int value) = 0;
};

template&lt;typename Base, typename A&gt;
struct Adapeter : public Base {
    void func(int value) override {
        static_cast&lt;A*&gt;(this)-&gt;funcA(value, *this);
    }
};

class MultiObserver :
    public Adapeter&lt;SlotA, MultiObserver&gt;,
    public Adapeter&lt;SlotB, MultiObserver&gt;
{
public:

    void funcA(int value, SlotA&amp;)
    {
        aValue = value;
    }

    void funcA(int value, SlotB&amp;)
    {
        bValue = value;
    }

    int aValue = 0;
    int bValue = 0;
};

int main()
{
    MultiObserver mo;

    SlotA* a = &amp;mo;
    a-&gt;func(1);

    SlotB* b = &amp;mo;
    b-&gt;func(2);
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/r8bYMdYYj" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/r8bYMdYYj</a></p>
<p>Note that <code>funcA</code> was used only to avoid warning about hiding virtual function by the overloads of <code>func</code>: <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/MhGGTG4cG" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/MhGGTG4cG</a></p>
<p>Why MSVC has this extra capability?<br />
I <strong>suspect</strong> (do not have prove for that) that this is side effect of C# features. In <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/interfaces/explicit-interface-implementation" rel="nofollow noreferrer">C# you can override in different way functions</a> with same name from different ancestors. Besides C# Microsoft provides C++CLI which is compatible with C# and <code>.Net</code> and most probably this feature was introduced to C++ compiler since it is present in C++CLI.</p>
